SUMMARY:Library Speakers Consortium - Exile\, Memory\, and the Meaning of Home: A Conversation with Ana Hebra Flaster
DESCRIPTION:You must register here to attend:  
 https://libraryc.org/nutleypubliclibrary/225105\n\n\n\nWhat happens when 
 the country you love forces you out\, leaving you just 48 hours to abandon 
 everything you’ve ever known? Join us online for an extraordinary online 
 conversation with author and essayist Ana Hebra Flaster as we discuss her 
 sweeping yet deeply intimate memoir Property of the Revolution: From a 
 Cuban Barrio to a New Hampshire Mill Town.\n\nAna Hebra Flaster was six 
 years old when her working-class family was kicked out of their Havana 
 barrio for opposing communism. Once devoted revolutionaries themselves but 
 disillusioned by the Castro government’s repressive tactics\, they fled 
 to the US. The permanent losses they suffered—of home\, country\, and 
 loved ones\, all within forty-eight hours—haunted her multigenerational 
 family as they reclaimed their lives and freedom in 1967 New Hampshire. 
 There\, they fed each other stories of their scrappy barrio—some of which 
 Hebra Flaster has shared on All Things Considered—to resurrect their lost 
 world and fortify themselves for a daunting task: building a new life in a 
 foreign land.\n\nWeaving pivotal events in Cuba–US history with her 
 viejos’—elders’—stories of surviving political upheaval\, 
 impossible choices\, and “refugeedom\,” Property of the Revolution 
 celebrates the indomitable spirit and wisdom of the women warriors who led 
 the family out of Cuba\, shaped its rebirth as Cuban Americans\, and helped 
 Ana grow up hopeful\, future-facing—American. But what happens when 
 deeply buried childhood memories resurface\, demanding an adult’s 
 reckoning?\n\nHere’s how the fiercest love\, the most stubborn will\, and 
 the power of family put nine new Americans back on their feet.\n\nRegister 
 today to gain a deeper\, more personal perspective on the refugee 
 experience and the enduring quest to define home.\n\nAbout the Author: 
 \n\nAna Hebra Flaster is journalist and the author of Property of the 
 Revolution: From a Cuban Barrio to a New Hampshire Mill Town\, published in 
 April 2025 (She Writes Press\, Simon and Schuster distribution). A former 
 child-refugee from Cuba\, Ana’s work has appeared in national print and 
 online media\, including: The New York Times\, The Wall Street Journal\, 
 The Washington Post\, and The Boston Globe. Her commentaries and 
 storytelling have aired on NPR’s All Things Considered and PBS’s 
 Stories from the Stage. Since its publication in April 2025\, Property of 
 the Revolution has won first place in several international writing 
 contests\, including: 2026 IBPA Book Awards (Memoir)\; 2025 Discovery Book 
 Awards (Nonfiction overall)\; 2025 International Book Awards (Creative 
 Nonfiction)\, and the 2025 IAP Book Awards (Memoir of the Year). The memoir 
 was selected as the Nashua\, New Hampshire\, 2026 All-City Read and the 
 Rivier University 2025 First-Year Read. Ana speaks regularly with student\, 
 library\, and civic groups about her family’s journey and the refugee and 
 immigrant experience. She is currently focused on covering the struggle for 
 democracy and human rights in Cuba in her weekly Substack\, CubaCurious\, a 
 project she launched in July 2024.\n\n\n\n
